###### 3.3 Using Package [*QuadGK*](https://juliapackages.com/p/quadgk)

Here, with code of package [*QuadGK.jl*](https://juliapackages.com/p/quadgk) we derive all *first-order* antiderivatives of the *Scotch-Yoke-Oscillator(SYO)* in *parallel*. A first-order antderivative in the context of *SYO* is based on the theoretical correct *state* function $f_x(τ) = x(τ) = cos(τ)$, $f_v(τ) = v(τ) = sin(τ)$, $f_a(τ) = a(τ) = -cos(τ)$, $f_j(τ) = j(τ) = -sin(τ)$. 

Then also with code of package [*QuadGK.jl*](https://juliapackages.com/p/quadgk) starting with $f_j(τ) = j(τ)$ we compute all *higher-order* (=*iterated*, *repeated*) antiderivatives $F_a(j(τ)), F_v(F_a(f_j(τ))), F_x(F_v(F_a(f_j(τ))))$. 

"

# ╔═╡ 7903f5f0-c098-450d-be16-e598813820a3
let Δτ = 1.0E-2
	τUpLim  =  2.0pi
	τs      =  [τ for τ in 0.0+Δτ/2:Δτ:τUpLim-Δτ/2] 
	jerk(τ) =  sin(τ)
	acc(τ)  = -cos(τ) # acceleration
	vel(τ)  = -sin(τ) # velocity
	pos(τ)  =  cos(τ) # position 
	Fa(τ)   =  quadgk(jerk, -1.55, τ)[1]
	Fv(τ)   =  quadgk(acc,  -0.0, τ)[1]
	Fx(τ)   =  quadgk(vel,   1.55, τ)[1]
	#------------------------------------------------------------------------------
	plot(xlimits=[-0.02, 10.52], ylimits=[-1.12, 1.26], title=L"QuadGK:Scotch-Yoke-Osc.\ (normalized, parallel)")
	#------------------------------------------------------------------------------
	plot!(τs, jerk, lw=1.0, color=:red, label=L"theoretical\ j(τ)")
	plot!(τs, acc,  lw=1.0, color=:green, label=L"theoretical\ a(t)")
	plot!(τs, vel,  lw=1.0, color=:orange, label=L"theoretical\ v(t)")
	plot!(τs, pos,  lw=1.0, color=:blue, label=L"theoretical\ x(t)")
	#------------------------------------------------------------------------------
	plot!([τ for τ in 0:Δτ:2π], Fa, linestyle=:dashdot, lw=2, color=:green, label=L"F_a(τ) \approx  c +\Delta x \sum_{i=0}^n f_j[i]")
	plot!([τ for τ in 0:Δτ:2π], Fv, linestyle=:dashdot, lw=2, color=:orange, label=L"F_v(τ) \approx c + \Delta x \sum_{i=0}^n f_a[i]")
	plot!([τ for τ in 0:Δτ:2π], Fx, linestyle=:dashdot, lw=2, color=:blue, label=L"F_x(τ) \approx c + \Delta x \sum_{i=0}^n f_v[i]")
	#------------------------------------------------------------------------------
end # let 

# ╔═╡ 975a8a2b-1dc6-4360-95f6-daf1d471b316
let Δτ      =  1.0E-2
	τUpLim  =  2.0pi
	τs      =  [τ for τ in 0.0+Δτ/2:Δτ:τUpLim-Δτ/2] 
	jerk(τ) =  sin(τ)
	acc(τ)  = -cos(τ) # acceleration
	vel(τ)  = -sin(τ) # velocity
	pos(τ)  =  cos(τ) # position 
	Fa(τ)   =  quadgk(jerk, -1.55, τ)[1]
	Fv(τ)   =  quadgk(Fa,   -0.0, τ)[1]
	Fx(τ)   =  quadgk(Fv,    1.6, τ)[1]
	#------------------------------------------------------------------------------
	plot(xlimits=[-0.02, 10.52], ylimits=[-1.12, 1.26], title=L"QuadGK:Scotch-Yoke-Oscillator\ (normalized, iterated)")
	#------------------------------------------------------------------------------
	plot!(τs, jerk, lw=1.0, color=:red, label=L"theoretical\ j(τ)")
	plot!(τs, acc,  lw=1.0, color=:green, label=L"theoretical\ a(t)")
	plot!(τs, vel,  lw=1.0, color=:orange, label=L"theoretical\ v(t)")
	plot!(τs, pos,  lw=1.0, color=:blue, label=L"theoretical\ x(t)")
	#------------------------------------------------------------------------------
	plot!([τ for τ in 0:Δτ:2π], τ->Fa(τ), linestyle=:dashdot, lw=2, color=:green, label=L"F_a(τ)=a(τ)")
	plot!([τ for τ in 0:Δτ:2π], τ->Fv(τ), linestyle=:dashdot, lw=2, color=:orange, label=L"F_v(τ) \approx c + \Delta x \sum_{i=0}^n F_a[i]")
	plot!([τ for τ in 0:Δτ:2π], τ->Fx(τ), linestyle=:dashdot, lw=2, color=:blue, label=L"F_x(τ) \approx c + \Delta x \sum_{i=0}^n F_v[i]")
	#------------------------------------------------------------------------------
end # let 

# ╔═╡ 7a8e9aaa-0fdb-43d0-841b-bdad8c1a6eea
md"
---
##### 4. Summary
We presented all *antiderivatives* of state function $j(τ), a(τ), v(τ), x(τ)$ in the context of *Scotch-Yoke-Oscillator* with *two* approaches. The first was *parallel* and the second was *iterated*. Both approaches were implemented by own and the with package $QuadGK$ code. The puzzling result is that our code results better than $QuadGK$'s. We have to study the reason for this with more care.
" 

